// src/elevate_gate.pdx — pdxping.M3-001 (#6): R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL
// elevate gate (WEAK, fail-closed).
//
// Scope
// -----
// This module implements pdxping#6 (M3-001): "R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL
// elevate request (fail-closed per §5.4)." Before any real ICMP send
// (M2-001 is out of scope for this cohort -- see CHANGELOG.md), a
// non-`--dry-run` invocation of pdxping calls
// `pdxping_elevate_check_and_require` exactly once. On denial (the
// only reachable outcome at this landing), `src/entry.pdx` writes
// `[pdxping.M3-001 EACCES]` to fd 2 and exits 13.
//
// Why this is a WEAK stub, and why that is the CORRECT answer today
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// The task brief for this issue asks for
// `elevate_client_acquire` -> `elevate_client_require` against the
// `R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL` right (mirrors `rm`'s own LE-001 (#29)
// acquire/require composition against `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL`, and
// `mount.pdxfs`'s / `umount.pdxfs`'s own `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL`
// fail-closed stubs). A full read of `libpdx-elevate`'s real API
// (`ElevateClientAcquire::elevate_client_acquire(caps, dur, req_buf,
// reply_ep_id, reply_buf, mint_ctx_buf) -> row_id | error`) finds the
// same prerequisite every one of those sibling repos' own module
// headers documents: the caller must already hold a `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT`
// cap over `svc.elevate-broker` at `mint_ctx_buf.parent_ep_slot`. This
// repo's `caps.decl` declares `KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL` as a PLACEHOLDER
// ONLY (see that file's own comment) -- no code path in this landing
// holds or invokes a real broker-endpoint cap, so there is no honest
// `mint_ctx_buf.parent_ep_slot` value this repo could populate. Rather
// than fabricate a plausible-looking `elevate_client_acquire` call
// against a field this repo cannot honestly fill in (the exact trap
// `mount.pdxfs`'s `mount_elev_require_system` and `rm`'s own module
// headers document avoiding), `pdxping_elevate_check_and_require`
// below always returns `EG_DENY`. This is the honest, fail-closed
// answer to "can this process prove it holds elevation over
// R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL": since no real elevation request can be
// constructed at all, "cannot prove GRANT" and "DENY" are the same
// externally observable outcome.
//
// This is ALSO not merely a repo-local gap papered over by a
// convenient stub: the kernel-side gate backing this exact right
// (`cap_check_r_net_privileged_protocol`,
// `src/kernel/core/cap/cap_net_privileged.pdx`, R100-PREP-003
// paideia-os#2009) is documented, AT HEAD, to permit only the
// boot-witness (`task_ptr == 0`) or `pid == 1` (init) -- i.e. even a
// hypothetical real `elevate_client_acquire`/`_require` round-trip from
// an ordinary ring-3 `pdxping` process would be refused `-EPERM`
// kernel-side today, independent of whether a broker-endpoint cap were
// ever provisioned to this tool. `EG_DENY` here is therefore not a
// pessimistic guess about an unknowable future state -- it matches the
// live kernel policy exactly. `EG_GRANT` is kept as a real, reachable
// return value (not deleted) so the day BOTH (a) a broker-endpoint cap
// is provisioned to this tool and (b) the kernel gate above is opened
// to ordinary ring-3 callers, only this file's body needs to change
// to a real `elevate_client_acquire` + `elevate_client_require`
// sequence -- `src/entry.pdx`'s call site does not change.
//
// paideia-as compliance
// ---------------------
//   - Module ElevateGate (PascalCase basename).
//   - Zero-argument function; trivially within the <= 4 curried-args
//     cap.
//   - No test mnemonic, no cmp at all in the WEAK-stub body (nothing
//     to branch on).
//   - Leaf; no push/pop (writes only rax).
//
// Upstream: design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §5.4 (paideia-os,
// the R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL elevate-class recommendation this
// issue implements); src/kernel/core/cap/cap_net_privileged.pdx
// (paideia-os, R100-PREP-003 #2009 -- the live kernel-side gate this
// stub's fail-closed posture matches); rm's src/elevate.pdx (LE-001,
// rm#29 -- the acquire/require composition pattern this file names but
// does not yet invoke); mount.pdxfs's src/elevate.pdx
// (`mount_elev_require_system` -- the identical "declared cap,
// unprovisioned broker, always-DENY" precedent this file's posture is
// adapted from).

module ElevateGate = structure {

  // --- Return codes. ------------------------------------------------------
  pub let EG_DENY  : u64 = 0
  pub let EG_GRANT : u64 = 1

  // --- Documentation-only mirror of the kernel-side right constant
  //     (src/kernel/core/cap/cap_net_privileged.pdx's
  //     R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL = 0x1000). Not consumed by the
  //     WEAK-stub body below -- kept so `grep R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL`
  //     across both repos surfaces this call site. -----------------------
  pub let R_NET_PRIVILEGED_PROTOCOL : u64 = 0x1000

  // ============================================================================
  // pdxping_elevate_check_and_require() -> u64
  //
  //   rax = EG_DENY (0) -- the only reachable value at this landing.
  //         EG_GRANT (1) is a real, wired return value with no live
  //         code path that produces it (see file header).
  //
  // Zero-argument WEAK stub. Called once by src/entry.pdx before any
  // non---dry-run echo attempt.
  // ============================================================================
  pub let pdxping_elevate_check_and_require : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "pdxping.M3-001 (#6) pdxping_elevate_check_and_require. WEAK, fail-closed stub -- see file header for the full precedent chain (rm LE-001 #29, mount.pdxfs mount_elev_require_system, umount.pdxfs elevate_request_system_unmount) and for why EG_DENY matches the live kernel-side cap_check_r_net_privileged_protocol gate (R100-PREP-003, paideia-os#2009) exactly rather than merely guessing pessimistically. Body: xor rax,rax; ret -- sets EG_DENY (0), touches no memory despite the declared !{mem} effect (the wider declaration matches what a real elevate_client_acquire/_require pair would require, per this codebase's convention of declaring the FUTURE real body's effect/capability set so the swap-to-real landing changes only body bytes, not the pub-let signature -- see pdxsock's own AuditWire module header for the identical convention applied to a sibling WEAK stub). Zero-argument function; leaf; no push/pop; writes only rax. No test mnemonic (no branch in this body at all). Labels: none (straight-line body).",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
